/* PsychLogic GATE 2027 · Subject Mock 1 · Psychological Foundations & Research Methods
   65 questions · 100 marks · Q1–30 carry 1 mark, Q31–65 carry 2 marks (question types mixed within each block) */
window.PL_TEST = {
  id: "mock-1",
  title: "Subject Mock 1: Psychological Foundations & Research Methods",
  sections: [{"id":"XH5","name":"XH5 Psychology"}],
  questions: [
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Hypotheses","q":"“There will be no significant difference between the memory scores of the two groups” is an example of a:","options":["Directional hypothesis","Alternative (research) hypothesis","Null hypothesis","Post-hoc hypothesis"],"answer":2,"why":"The null hypothesis (H₀) states that there is no difference or no relationship. Statistical tests are run to decide whether H₀ can be rejected. Researchers never ‘prove’ the alternative hypothesis directly. They test whether the data are unlikely enough under H₀ to reject it.","whyNot":["A directional hypothesis predicts which group will score higher.","The alternative hypothesis predicts that a difference or relationship exists.","","A post-hoc hypothesis is formed after looking at the data."],"related":["Rejecting a true H₀ is a Type I error.","A non-directional alternative hypothesis leads to a two-tailed test."],"watch":"Hypotheses, especially the null hypothesis, keep appearing in statement-type questions. Know its exact wording.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Designing research","q":"A researcher defines <i>test anxiety</i> as “the total score a student obtains on the Westside Test Anxiety Scale”. This step in designing the study is called:","options":["Conceptualisation","Operational definition","Hypothesis formulation","Construction of a sampling frame"],"answer":1,"why":"An operational definition states a variable in terms of the exact procedure used to measure it. Here, test anxiety means its score on one particular scale. A clear operational definition tells other researchers exactly how to measure the variable, so the study can be repeated and checked.","whyNot":["Conceptualisation gives the abstract, dictionary-like meaning of a construct.","","A hypothesis is a testable statement about how variables are related.","A sampling frame is the list from which a sample is drawn."],"related":["Operational definitions make studies replicable.","Conceptual definition comes first, operational definition next."],"watch":"Operational definition is a favourite 1-mark question. One question from research design is almost certain every year.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Social Psychology","topic":"Attribution","level":"H","q":"Ravi sees a stranger trip on the stairs and thinks, “How clumsy!”. When Ravi trips at the same spot, he blames the wet floor. This best illustrates the:","options":["Actor–observer bias","Fundamental attribution error","Self-serving bias","False consensus effect"],"answer":0,"why":"Explaining <i>others’</i> behaviour by their disposition but <i>our own</i> by the situation is the actor–observer bias. Only the comparison between the two situations makes this answer exact.","whyNot":["","The FAE covers only the judgement of the stranger. It does not explain the contrast with Ravi’s own fall.","The self-serving bias is about success and failure (credit vs blame), not a plain accident.","The false consensus effect is overestimating how much others share our views."],"related":["Jones and Nisbett (1971) described the actor–observer bias.","The fundamental attribution error was named by Ross (1977)."],"watch":"When a question contrasts ‘self’ and ‘others’, think actor–observer, not FAE."},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Humanistic perspective","q":"Which of the following is/are associated with the <b>humanistic</b> perspective?","options":["Unconditional positive regard","Self-actualisation","Unconscious conflicts","Free will"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"Humanistic psychology (Maslow, Rogers) sees people as free agents striving to grow and reach their potential. Self-actualisation, free will and unconditional positive regard are central ideas. Humanistic psychologists believed every person has an inborn tendency to grow, and that the right supportive conditions let this potential unfold.","whyNot":["","","Unconscious conflict is the core of the psychodynamic perspective.",""],"related":["Rogers developed client-centred therapy.","Maslow put self-actualisation at the top of his hierarchy of needs."],"watch":"Perspectives and their view of human nature are a recurring match topic. Revise all major perspectives.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Demand characteristics","level":"H","q":"In a ‘study of advertising’, participants guess that the researcher hopes they will prefer Brand X, so they rate Brand X highly. Their ratings are mainly distorted by:","options":["Social desirability bias","Practice effect","Experimenter expectancy effect","Demand characteristics"],"answer":3,"why":"Demand characteristics are cues in the study that let participants guess the hypothesis and act to ‘help’ the researcher (Orne, 1962). The distortion comes from what participants think is wanted in the study, not from wanting to look good.","whyNot":["Social desirability is about presenting oneself favourably in general, not about guessing the hypothesis.","A practice effect is improvement from repeating a task.","The experimenter expectancy effect comes from the researcher’s own behaviour influencing results.",""],"related":["Cover stories and single-blind procedures reduce demand characteristics.","Social desirability is reduced by anonymous responding."],"watch":"Social desirability vs demand characteristics is a close distinction. Ask whether the participant is guessing the study’s aim."},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Experimental control","q":"In a <b>single-blind</b> drug trial, who does not know which participants receive the active drug?","options":["Both the participants and the experimenter","Only the experimenter","Neither the participants nor the experimenter","Only the participants"],"answer":3,"why":"In a single-blind design only the participants are kept unaware of their condition. This controls placebo effects and participants’ expectations. Because participants do not know whether they got the drug or a placebo, any improvement caused only by expectation shows up equally in both groups and cancels out.","whyNot":["When both are unaware, the design is double-blind. It also controls experimenter bias.","This is not the standard meaning of single-blind.","That describes an open (unblinded) trial.",""],"related":["Double-blind designs also control experimenter expectancy (the Rosenthal effect).","A placebo is an inactive treatment given to the control group."],"watch":"Blinding (single and double) is a repeatedly tested control technique. Expect one question on experimental control.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Observation","q":"Which of the following is/are true of <b>naturalistic observation</b>?","options":["Behaviour is observed in its natural setting","It can be affected by observer bias","The researcher manipulates the independent variable","It cannot by itself establish cause and effect"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"In naturalistic observation the researcher watches behaviour where it normally happens, without interfering. Because nothing is manipulated, it cannot prove causation, and the observer’s expectations can bias the records. Its main strength is high ecological validity, because behaviour is seen as it naturally happens.","whyNot":["","","Manipulating the IV is the mark of an experiment.",""],"related":["Inter-observer reliability reduces observer bias.","Participant observation is used in ethnography."],"watch":"Data-collection methods are a stable 1-mark area.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Schools of psychology","q":"Wilhelm Wundt : Structuralism :: William James : ________","options":["Functionalism","Gestalt psychology","Behaviourism","Psychoanalysis"],"answer":0,"why":"James asked what the mind is <i>for</i>, meaning how mental processes help us adapt to the environment. This approach is functionalism, influenced by Darwin. Structuralism (Wundt/Titchener) asked what the mind is <i>made of</i>. Structuralism tried to break consciousness into basic elements (sensations, images, feelings). Functionalism asked how these processes help the organism survive, which later shaped educational and applied psychology.","whyNot":["","Gestalt was founded by Wertheimer, Köhler and Koffka.","Behaviourism was founded by John B. Watson (1913).","Psychoanalysis was founded by Sigmund Freud."],"related":["James wrote <i>The Principles of Psychology</i> (1890).","John Dewey and James Angell were other functionalists."],"watch":"Analogy questions (A : B :: C : ?) have started appearing in recent papers. Expect one or two, and learn every founder–school pair.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Psychometrics","topic":"Reliability","q":"Which of the following is/are methods of estimating the <b>reliability</b> of a test?","options":["Content validation","Split-half method","Test–retest method","Alternate (parallel) forms method"],"answer":[1,2,3],"why":"Reliability means consistency. Test–retest, split-half and parallel forms all estimate consistency of scores. Each method checks consistency in a different way: over time (test–retest), across forms (parallel forms) or within one test (split-half).","whyNot":["Content validation checks <i>validity</i>, meaning whether items cover the content domain.","","",""],"related":["The Spearman–Brown formula corrects split-half reliability to full-test length.","Cronbach’s alpha estimates internal consistency."],"watch":"Reliability vs validity is one of the most repeated psychometrics ideas. Expect at least one question.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Levels of measurement","q":"Students’ ranks in a class (1st, 2nd, 3rd …) are measured on which scale?","options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"answer":1,"why":"Ranks show order but not equal distances between positions, so they are ordinal data. The gap between 1st and 2nd may differ from the gap between 2nd and 3rd. We know who came first, second and third, but not by how much. That is exactly what ‘order without equal intervals’ means.","whyNot":["Nominal scales only name categories, for example blood group.","","Interval scales have equal units but no true zero, for example temperature in °C.","Ratio scales have equal units and a true zero, for example reaction time."],"related":["Stevens (1946) proposed the four levels of measurement.","Ordinal data are analysed with non-parametric tests such as Spearman’s rho."],"watch":"Levels of measurement decide which statistical test is used, so this idea keeps resurfacing. Revise it with non-parametric tests.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Research ethics","q":"Under the APA Ethical Principles, which of the following is/are <b>acceptable</b> practice(s) in research with human participants?","options":["Withholding the exact hypothesis until the end, when this causes no harm","Replacing the data of drop-outs with estimated scores without reporting it","Continuing to record data from a participant who has asked to withdraw","Debriefing participants after a study that involved deception"],"answer":[0,3],"why":"Debriefing is required after deception. Temporarily withholding the hypothesis is allowed when it is justified and harmless. The aim of these rules is to protect participants’ dignity, rights and wellbeing while still allowing useful research.","whyNot":["","Unreported replacement of data is fabrication and a serious breach of research integrity.","Participants have the right to withdraw at any time without penalty.",""],"related":["Key principles: informed consent, confidentiality, right to withdraw, no harm, debriefing.","Deception is never allowed when it could cause pain or severe emotional distress."],"watch":"Research ethics has been asked in each of the last few years, often as an MSQ. One question is definitely expected.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Variables","q":"In a study of the effect of sleep deprivation on reaction time, <b>reaction time</b> is the:","options":["Independent variable","Extraneous variable","Moderator variable","Dependent variable"],"answer":3,"why":"The dependent variable (DV) is what is measured to see the effect of the independent variable (IV). Sleep deprivation is the IV, and reaction time is the DV. A quick way to decide: the IV is the ‘cause’ the researcher changes, and the DV is the ‘effect’ that gets measured. Here sleep loss is changed and reaction time is measured.","whyNot":["Sleep deprivation, not reaction time, is the variable that is manipulated.","An extraneous variable is an unwanted outside variable, such as noise in the room.","A moderator changes the strength or direction of the IV–DV relationship, for example age.",""],"related":["A confounding variable is an extraneous variable that changes along with the IV.","A mediator explains <i>how</i> the IV affects the DV."],"watch":"Variable identification from a short scenario is a common 1-mark pattern.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"History","level":"M","q":"Wundt’s method of introspection was later criticised mainly because:","options":["It was used only with animals","It ignored conscious experience altogether","Observers’ reports were private and could not be checked or agreed upon by others","It depended on large-scale surveys"],"answer":2,"why":"Introspection relied on trained observers describing their own conscious experience. Different laboratories got different results, and there was no way to verify a private report. Behaviourists (Watson) used this weakness to argue that psychology should study only observable behaviour.","whyNot":["Introspection was used with trained human observers, not animals.","Introspection was entirely about conscious experience.","","Surveys were not part of Wundt’s method."],"related":["Wundt set up the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879.","Titchener’s structuralism also used introspection."],"watch":"History of psychology is usually asked through ‘why’ and ‘what changed’, not just dates. Expect one such question."},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Qualitative research","q":"Which of the following is/are <b>qualitative</b> research approach(es)?","options":["Phenomenological study","Narrative analysis","Randomised controlled trial","Grounded theory"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"Grounded theory, phenomenology and narrative analysis all study meanings and experiences through words rather than numbers. These approaches aim at understanding meanings and experiences in depth, rather than measuring variables numerically.","whyNot":["","","A randomised controlled trial is a quantitative experimental design.",""],"related":["Other qualitative approaches are ethnography, case study and thematic analysis.","Qualitative data are usually collected through interviews, focus groups and observation."],"watch":"Quantitative vs qualitative research is one of the most consistently asked topics. Revise it thoroughly.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Learning & Memory","topic":"Operant conditioning","q":"Carrying an umbrella removes the chance of getting wet, so a person carries an umbrella more often. This is an example of:","options":["Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment"],"answer":1,"why":"Negative reinforcement means a behaviour increases because it <i>removes</i> something unpleasant. Getting wet is avoided, so the behaviour of carrying an umbrella increases. ‘Negative’ means something is removed, and ‘reinforcement’ means the behaviour increases. Removing the unpleasant experience of getting wet strengthens the habit of carrying an umbrella.","whyNot":["Positive reinforcement <i>adds</i> something pleasant.","","Positive punishment adds something unpleasant to decrease a behaviour.","Negative punishment removes something pleasant to decrease a behaviour."],"related":["Reinforcement always increases behaviour; punishment always decreases it.","Escape and avoidance learning are forms of negative reinforcement."],"watch":"Reinforcement and punishment scenarios appear almost every year. Practise deciding ‘add or remove’ and ‘increase or decrease’.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Central tendency","q":"Which measure of central tendency is most affected by a few extreme scores?","options":["Median","Mode","Mean","Inter-quartile range"],"answer":2,"why":"The mean uses every score in its calculation, so one very high or very low score pulls it strongly. The median depends only on the middle position. For example, in the scores 2, 3, 4, 5 and 86, the mean is 20 while the median stays at 4. The median describes the typical score far better here.","whyNot":["The median resists extreme scores. That is why it is used for skewed data such as income.","The mode is simply the most frequent score.","","The inter-quartile range measures dispersion, not central tendency."],"related":["In a positively skewed distribution, mean > median > mode.","In a normal distribution, mean = median = mode."],"watch":"Basic statistics shows up even in non-statistics mocks. GATE has 2–7 statistics questions every year.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Forces in psychology","level":"M","q":"A psychologist explains a client’s low mood as the result of blocked personal growth and a childhood in which love was given only on certain conditions. This explanation comes from the:","options":["Psychodynamic perspective (first force)","Behavioural perspective (second force)","Humanistic perspective (third force)","Biological perspective"],"answer":2,"why":"Blocked growth (self-actualisation) and conditional love (conditions of worth) are humanistic ideas from Rogers and Maslow, the ‘third force’ in psychology. The explanation focuses on the person’s potential, not on unconscious conflict or reinforcement history.","whyNot":["The psychodynamic view would point to unconscious conflicts and early drives.","The behavioural view would point to reinforcement and learning history.","","The biological view would point to the brain, genes or neurotransmitters."],"related":["Psychoanalysis is the first force and behaviourism the second.","Rogers called a positive, growth-promoting environment ‘unconditional positive regard’."],"watch":"Perspectives are now often asked through short cases. Identify the key word (growth, unconscious, reinforcement) to find the perspective."},
    {"type":"NAT","marks":1,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Standard error","q":"A population has a standard deviation of 12. For random samples of size 36, the standard error of the mean is ________ (answer in integer).","answer":{"min":2,"max":2},"why":"SE = σ ÷ √n = 12 ÷ √36 = 12 ÷ 6 = <b>2</b>. The SE tells us how much sample means would vary from sample to sample, so a smaller SE means the sample mean is a more accurate estimate of the population mean.","related":["SE shrinks as sample size grows.","SE is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the mean."],"watch":"Standard-error numericals have become regular. Practise the formula until it is automatic.","whyNot":[],"level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Ethnography","q":"In ethnographic research, <b>‘thick description’</b> refers to:","options":["Describing behaviour together with its context and the meaning it has for the group","Recording only observable behaviour in numerical form","Using a very long questionnaire","Writing about the researcher’s own emotions only"],"answer":0,"why":"Clifford Geertz (1973) used ‘thick description’ to mean explaining not just what people do but what it <i>means</i> in their culture. His example: telling a wink apart from a mere twitch. Thick description gives the reader the cultural meaning behind an action, so the behaviour can be understood from the insider’s point of view.","whyNot":["","That is ‘thin’, quantitative description.","Questionnaire length has nothing to do with it.","Reflexivity about the researcher is useful, but it is not thick description."],"related":["Ethnography involves long-term immersion and participant observation in a culture-sharing group.","Emic means the insider’s view; etic means the outsider’s view."],"watch":"Qualitative research terms are gaining weight in recent papers. Know ‘thick description’ and the name Geertz.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Experimental control","q":"Random assignment of participants to experimental and control groups mainly helps to:","options":["Increase the sample size","Make the groups equivalent at the start by spreading participant differences evenly","Make the results generalisable to the whole population","Remove the need for a control group"],"answer":1,"why":"Random assignment gives every participant an equal chance of being in any group. Individual differences are then spread evenly, which protects internal validity. By chance alone, differences such as intelligence or motivation become roughly equal in all groups. Any difference after the treatment can then be put down to the IV.","whyNot":["Sample size depends on how many people are recruited, not on assignment.","","Generalisability comes from random <i>sampling</i>, not random assignment.","A control group is still needed for comparison."],"related":["Random sampling relates to external validity; random assignment relates to internal validity.","Matching is another way to make groups equal."],"watch":"Confusing random sampling with random assignment is the most common trap. Expect it.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Gestalt psychology","level":"M","q":"Still frames of a film shown in quick succession are seen as smooth motion. Wertheimer used this kind of apparent motion to argue that:","options":["Perception is a simple reflex response","Sensations are elements joined together by association","Motion perception is entirely learned through reinforcement","Perception is organised as a whole and is not simply built up from separate sensations"],"answer":3,"why":"No single frame contains movement, yet we see motion. So the experience must come from how the mind organises the whole pattern. This phi phenomenon (1912) launched Gestalt psychology’s claim that the whole is different from the sum of its parts.","whyNot":["Apparent motion is a perceptual organisation, not a reflex.","This is the structuralist and associationist view that Gestalt psychologists rejected.","Gestalt psychologists stressed innate organising tendencies, not reinforcement.",""],"related":["Köhler and Koffka were Wertheimer’s co-founders.","Gestalt principles include proximity, similarity, closure and continuity."],"watch":"Gestalt appears both under schools of psychology and under perception. Be ready for either."},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Behaviourism","q":"Which of the following psychologist(s) is/are associated with <b>behaviourism</b>?","options":["Carl Rogers","Abraham Maslow","B. F. Skinner","John B. Watson"],"answer":[2,3],"why":"Watson founded behaviourism in 1913, and Skinner developed radical behaviourism and operant conditioning. Both said psychology should study observable behaviour. Behaviourists explained behaviour through conditioning and reinforcement, not inner mental states.","whyNot":["Rogers is a humanistic psychologist.","Maslow is a humanistic psychologist.","",""],"related":["Watson’s ‘Little Albert’ study (1920) showed conditioned fear.","Behaviourism dominated American psychology from about 1913 to the 1950s."],"watch":"Match psychologist to school. It is easy, but MSQs give no partial marks.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Data collection methods","level":"M","q":"Which of the following research problems is best suited to a <b>case study</b> rather than an experiment or survey?","options":["The effect of caffeine on reaction time in 100 students","A comparison of two teaching methods across 20 schools","The relationship between income and happiness across a country","The memory abilities of a patient who has had both hippocampi removed"],"answer":3,"why":"A rare, unique case cannot be created or repeated in an experiment, and there are too few such people for a survey. A detailed case study is the best way to learn from it, as with patient H.M.","whyNot":["This is suited to an experiment with a large sample.","This is suited to a field experiment or quasi-experiment.","This is suited to a large correlational survey.",""],"related":["Case studies are rich in detail but low in generalisability.","Famous case studies include Phineas Gage, H.M. and Little Hans."],"watch":"Choosing the right method for a research problem is a common application question."},
    {"type":"NAT","marks":1,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Median","q":"The median of the scores 7, 3, 9, 5, 11 and 15 is ________ (answer in integer).","answer":{"min":8,"max":8},"why":"Arrange them in order: 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 15. With 6 scores, the median is the average of the 3rd and 4th: (7 + 9) ÷ 2 = <b>8</b>. With an even number of scores there is no single middle score, so we take the average of the two middle scores.","related":["With an odd number of scores, the median is the middle score.","Always arrange the scores before finding the median."],"watch":"Don’t forget to sort the scores first. That is the trap in median questions.","whyNot":[],"level":"E"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Dispersion","q":"Which of the following is/are measures of <b>dispersion</b> (variability)?","options":["Quartile deviation","Median","Mean","Standard deviation"],"answer":[0,3],"why":"Standard deviation and quartile deviation describe how spread out the scores are. Averages tell us where the centre of the scores lies; measures of dispersion tell us how far the scores spread around that centre.","whyNot":["","The median is a measure of central tendency.","The mean is a measure of central tendency, not dispersion.",""],"related":["Variance is the square of the standard deviation.","Quartile deviation = (Q3 − Q1) ÷ 2."],"watch":"SD and variance calculations come as NAT questions almost every year.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Biological Psychology","topic":"Neurotransmitters","level":"M","q":"A patient develops a resting tremor, rigidity and slowed movement. The neurons most likely to have degenerated are those releasing:","options":["Acetylcholine","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine"],"answer":3,"why":"These are signs of Parkinson’s disease, caused by the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, which supply the basal ganglia that control smooth movement.","whyNot":["Loss of acetylcholine is linked with memory decline in Alzheimer’s disease.","Low GABA activity is linked with anxiety and seizures.","Serotonin is linked with mood, sleep and appetite.",""],"related":["L-DOPA, a dopamine precursor, is the main treatment.","Excess dopamine activity is linked with positive symptoms of schizophrenia."],"watch":"Neurotransmitter questions are increasingly framed through symptoms and disorders."},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Sampling","q":"Which of the following is/are <b>probability</b> sampling technique(s)?","options":["Stratified random sampling","Snowball sampling","Quota sampling","Cluster sampling"],"answer":[0,3],"why":"In probability sampling, every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected. Stratified and cluster sampling both use random selection. Only probability methods allow us to calculate sampling error and generalise statistically from the sample to the population.","whyNot":["","Snowball sampling is non-probability: participants refer others.","Quota sampling is non-probability: quotas are filled by convenience, with no random selection.",""],"related":["Simple random sampling: every member and every possible sample is equally likely.","Non-probability methods are convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling."],"watch":"Sampling is asked almost every year in some form: definition, MSQ or match.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Developmental designs","q":"A researcher compares the memory of 10-, 20- and 30-year-old people, all tested in the same month. The design is:","options":["Cross-sectional","Longitudinal","Cross-sequential","Case study"],"answer":0,"why":"In a cross-sectional design, different age groups are studied at one point in time. It is quick, but differences may be due to cohort (generation) effects. Because people of different ages also grew up at different times, differences between age groups may reflect their generation rather than age itself.","whyNot":["","A longitudinal design follows the <i>same</i> people over many years.","A cross-sequential design combines both: several age groups followed over time.","A case study is an in-depth study of one person."],"related":["Cohort effects are a weakness of cross-sectional designs.","Attrition (drop-out) is a weakness of longitudinal designs."],"watch":"Developmental research designs are asked regularly, often as scenarios. Learn to identify all three designs.","level":"E"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Expectancy effects","level":"M","q":"Teachers are told, falsely, that some randomly chosen pupils are ‘late bloomers’. By the end of the year those pupils show larger IQ gains than others. This is best described as the:","options":["Hawthorne effect","Placebo effect","Pygmalion (Rosenthal) effect","Halo effect"],"answer":2,"why":"The teachers’ raised expectations changed how they treated the pupils, and the pupils’ performance rose to match. Rosenthal and Jacobson (1968) called this the Pygmalion effect, a self-fulfilling prophecy.","whyNot":["The Hawthorne effect is a change in behaviour because people know they are being observed.","The placebo effect comes from a person’s own expectation of treatment.","","The halo effect is one good trait colouring our overall judgement of a person."],"related":["Expectancy effects are controlled by keeping experimenters ‘blind’.","The Hawthorne effect is a form of participant reactivity."],"watch":"Separating look-alike effects (Hawthorne, Pygmalion, placebo, halo) is a favourite trap."},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":1,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Correlational research","q":"Which of the following statement(s) about a <b>correlational</b> study is/are correct?","options":["It establishes that one variable causes the other","A third variable may explain the observed relationship","The relationship may be positive or negative","It shows how strongly two variables are related"],"answer":[1,2,3],"why":"Correlation measures the strength and direction of a relationship, from −1 to +1. It cannot prove causation, because a third variable or reverse direction may explain the link. For example, ice-cream sales and drowning both rise together because of a third variable, hot weather. Neither causes the other.","whyNot":["“Correlation does not imply causation.”","","",""],"related":["A coefficient of 0 means no linear relationship.","Spurious correlation is a link produced by a third variable."],"watch":"Correlational research comes up again and again. Remember that correlation never proves causation.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Psychometrics","topic":"Validity","q":"Which of the following is/are types of <b>validity</b>?","options":["Internal consistency","Split-half validity","Content validity","Criterion-related validity"],"answer":[2,3],"why":"Content validity and criterion-related validity are types of validity. (Construct validity is the third classic type.) Validity asks whether the test measures what it claims to measure, which is different from consistency (reliability).","whyNot":["Internal consistency (for example Cronbach’s alpha) is a type of reliability, not validity.","Split-half is a method of estimating reliability.","",""],"related":["Face validity is how valid a test looks to test-takers. It is not a technical type.","Reliability sets the upper limit for validity."],"watch":"Validity types and coefficients are tested frequently. One psychometrics question on validity is very likely.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Biological Psychology","topic":"Brain structures","q":"Match the brain structure in Column I with its main function in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Medulla</td><td>(i) Balance and coordination of movement</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Cerebellum</td><td>(ii) Hunger, thirst and body temperature</td></tr><tr><td>R. Hippocampus</td><td>(iii) Breathing and heart rate</td></tr><tr><td>S. Hypothalamus</td><td>(iv) Forming new long-term memories</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(i), Q-(iii), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)","P-(ii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(iii)"],"answer":0,"why":"The medulla controls vital functions (iii). The cerebellum coordinates movement (i). The hippocampus consolidates new memories (iv). The hypothalamus regulates drives (ii). The medulla and hypothalamus control basic survival functions, while the hippocampus and cerebellum support learning (memory and motor skills).","whyNot":["Medulla and cerebellum are swapped.","The hippocampus does not control balance.","The hypothalamus does not control breathing."],"related":["Patient H.M. lost the ability to form new memories after his hippocampi were removed.","The lateral hypothalamus starts eating; the ventromedial hypothalamus stops it."],"watch":"Brain-structure match questions appear regularly. Revise the functions of the major structures.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"NAT","marks":2,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Standard deviation","q":"The standard deviation of the scores 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 (treat them as a population, divide by N) is ________ (answer in integer).","answer":{"min":2,"max":2},"why":"Mean = 40 ÷ 8 = 5. The squared deviations are 9, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 4, 16, which sum to 32. Variance = 32 ÷ 8 = 4, so SD = √4 = <b>2</b>. Work step by step: find the mean, subtract it from each score, square the differences, average them to get the variance, then take the square root.","related":["Variance is the square of SD.","Dividing by N − 1 gives the sample (unbiased) estimate."],"watch":"Standard-deviation numericals are among the most predictable NATs. Practise them on the virtual calculator.","whyNot":[],"level":"H"},
    {"type":"NAT","marks":2,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Type I and Type II errors","q":"A researcher sets α = 0.01 and wants the power of the test to be 0.80. The ratio β / α is ________ (answer in integer).","answer":{"min":20,"max":20},"why":"Power = 1 − β, so β = 1 − 0.80 = 0.20. β / α = 0.20 ÷ 0.01 = <b>20</b>. Remember the link: α is the risk of a false alarm and β is the risk of missing a real effect. Power is the chance of detecting a real effect.","related":["Lowering α raises β if everything else is unchanged.","Conventional power is 0.80 (Cohen)."],"watch":"α–β numericals have become a pattern. Practise them; there is no negative marking on NATs.","whyNot":[],"level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Sampling","q":"Match the sampling technique in Column I with its description in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Quota sampling</td><td>(i) Every <i>k</i>-th person is chosen from a list after a random start</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Snowball sampling</td><td>(ii) Fixed numbers from each category are filled by convenience</td></tr><tr><td>R. Systematic sampling</td><td>(iii) Whole natural groups (such as schools) are chosen at random</td></tr><tr><td>S. Cluster sampling</td><td>(iv) Existing participants recruit further participants</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iii), R-(i), S-(iv)","P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(i), Q-(iv), R-(ii), S-(iii)"],"answer":0,"why":"Quota fills set numbers by convenience (ii). Snowball uses referrals (iv). Systematic picks every k-th name (i). Cluster selects whole groups at random (iii). The key difference is whether random selection is involved. Systematic and cluster sampling use it; quota and snowball sampling do not.","whyNot":["","Snowball is not about choosing whole groups.","Quota sampling does not depend on referrals.","Quota sampling is not ‘every k-th’ selection."],"related":["Snowball sampling suits hidden populations, for example drug users.","In cluster sampling, the group (not the person) is the unit of random selection."],"watch":"Sampling appears every year in some format: definition, MSQ or match.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Reactivity","q":"Which of the following is/are examples of <b>participant reactivity</b>?","options":["Respondents describe themselves in a socially desirable way","Scores fall because the test is taken on a very hot afternoon","Workers raise their output only because they know they are being observed","Participants guess the hypothesis and behave so as to confirm it"],"answer":[0,2,3],"why":"Reactivity means people change their behaviour <i>because they are being studied</i>. Examples are the Hawthorne effect, demand characteristics and social desirability. All three are cases where being studied changes how people respond, so the data no longer show natural behaviour.","whyNot":["","Heat is an environmental (extraneous) variable, not a reaction to being studied.","",""],"related":["Unobtrusive measures and cover stories reduce reactivity.","Orne (1962) described demand characteristics."],"watch":"Reactivity links to errors in measurement, a recurring theme in both research and psychometrics.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Repeated measures","q":"In a repeated-measures (within-subjects) design the same participants take part in all conditions. The main problem this creates and its usual remedy are:","options":["Selection bias; random sampling","Order (carry-over) effects; counterbalancing","Experimenter bias; double-blind procedure","Attrition; a larger sample"],"answer":1,"why":"Performing one condition can affect performance in the next, through practice, fatigue or carry-over. Counterbalancing (for example ABBA or a Latin square) spreads these order effects evenly. Counterbalancing means different participants receive the conditions in different orders, so order effects cancel out across the whole sample.","whyNot":["Selection bias is a problem of between-groups designs.","","Experimenter bias is not specific to repeated measures.","Attrition mainly worries longitudinal studies."],"related":["Advantage of repeated measures: fewer participants needed and individual differences are controlled.","Repeated-measures ANOVA analyses such data."],"watch":"Repeated-measures designs are named in the syllabus and appear in design and ANOVA questions.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Social Psychology","topic":"Conformity","q":"In Asch-type conformity experiments, conformity drops most sharply when:","options":["The group size increases from 3 to 15","The task becomes more ambiguous","One other group member gives the correct answer, breaking unanimity","The participant has to answer aloud in front of the group"],"answer":2,"why":"Asch found that a single dissenter who gave the right answer reduced conformity dramatically. Unanimity of the majority is the key condition. Once unanimity is broken, participants feel less social pressure and gain confidence in their own judgement.","whyNot":["Conformity rises with group size only up to about 3–4 people, then levels off; it does not drop.","Ambiguity <i>increases</i> conformity, through informational influence.","","Public answering <i>increases</i> conformity."],"related":["Normative influence comes from wanting to be liked; informational influence from wanting to be right.","Asch’s line-judgement studies were published in 1951–56."],"watch":"Conformity research is a steady social psychology topic. Know what increases and what reduces conformity.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Motivation & Emotion","topic":"Arousal","q":"Which of the following statement(s) about the <b>Yerkes–Dodson law</b> is/are correct?","options":["The optimal level of arousal is lower for difficult tasks","Very high arousal can lower performance","The optimal level of arousal is higher for complex tasks than for simple tasks","Performance improves with arousal up to an optimal level"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"Performance rises with arousal up to an optimum and then falls (an inverted U). Difficult tasks need lower arousal, so the option claiming the opposite is wrong.","whyNot":["","","It is the reverse: complex tasks are performed best at <i>lower</i> arousal.",""],"related":["Arousal theory says we seek an optimal level of arousal.","Simple tasks are done best at fairly high arousal."],"watch":"Arousal and performance (Yerkes–Dodson) is a recurring motivation question.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Interviews","q":"Which of the following statement(s) about a <b>structured interview</b> is/are correct?","options":["The interviewer freely changes questions to follow interesting leads","The same questions are asked in the same order to every respondent","It usually has higher reliability than an unstructured interview","Answers are easier to compare and quantify"],"answer":[1,2,3],"why":"Structured interviews use a fixed schedule. This standardisation makes answers comparable and reliable, but less flexible. The fixed format reduces interviewer influence, but it can miss unexpected or deeper information that a flexible interview would capture.","whyNot":["That describes an unstructured interview.","","",""],"related":["Semi-structured interviews have a guide but allow follow-up questions.","Depth interviews are unstructured and exploratory."],"watch":"Types of interview are a recurring option in method questions. Know structured, semi-structured and depth interviews.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Experimental control","q":"Which of the following is/are methods of controlling extraneous variables in an experiment?","options":["Holding conditions constant across groups","Random assignment of participants","Increasing demand characteristics","Counterbalancing the order of conditions"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"Randomisation, constancy and counterbalancing are standard control techniques. Each one prevents an unwanted variable from changing along with the IV. Each method stops an unwanted variable from changing along with the IV, so differences in the DV can be put down to the IV alone.","whyNot":["","","Demand characteristics are a <i>problem</i> to be reduced, not a control.",""],"related":["Elimination (for example a sound-proof room) is another control method.","Matching and blocking are also used."],"watch":"Questions on how to control confounds go along with questions on validity.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Single-subject design","q":"A therapist records a child’s tantrums at baseline (A), during a reward programme (B), after removing the programme (A), and after re-introducing it (B). This design is a:","options":["Multiple-baseline design","Factorial design","Reversal (withdrawal) ABAB design","Cohort design"],"answer":2,"why":"In the ABAB reversal design the treatment is introduced, withdrawn and introduced again. If behaviour changes each time, the treatment is shown to be the cause. If tantrums fall during each B phase and rise again during the A phase, it is very likely that the reward programme caused the change.","whyNot":["A multiple-baseline design starts the treatment at different times across behaviours, settings or people.","A factorial design studies two or more IVs together.","","A cohort design follows a group born in the same period."],"related":["Multiple-baseline designs are used when reversal is unethical.","Single-subject designs are common in behaviour modification."],"watch":"Single-subject designs are listed in the GATE syllabus and come up regularly in research-design questions.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Hypothesis testing","q":"Match the term in Column I with its meaning in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Type I error</td><td>(i) 1 − β</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Type II error</td><td>(ii) Rejecting a null hypothesis that is true</td></tr><tr><td>R. Power</td><td>(iii) The probability of a Type I error that the researcher is willing to accept</td></tr><tr><td>S. Level of significance (α)</td><td>(iv) Retaining a null hypothesis that is false</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)"],"answer":1,"why":"A Type I error is a false rejection (ii). A Type II error is a false retention (iv). Power = 1 − β (i). α is the chosen probability of a Type I error (iii). The two errors pull against each other: making α stricter reduces Type I errors but increases the risk of Type II errors, unless the sample size is increased.","whyNot":["Type I and Type II are swapped.","","Power and α are swapped.","A Type I error is not the same thing as α."],"related":["A Type I error is like a ‘false alarm’; a Type II error is like a ‘miss’.","Power rises with larger samples and larger effects."],"watch":"Type I and Type II errors are asked almost every year, as MCQ, MSQ or numerical. A question here is very likely.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Hypotheses","q":"Which of the following statement(s) about a research hypothesis is/are correct?","options":["The null hypothesis states that there is no difference or no relationship","A directional hypothesis is tested with a two-tailed test","It may be directional or non-directional","It is a testable prediction about variables"],"answer":[0,2,3],"why":"A hypothesis is a tentative, testable statement. It can be directional (one-tailed test) or non-directional (two-tailed test), and the null hypothesis predicts no effect. The tricky option swaps the type of test.","whyNot":["","A directional hypothesis uses a <i>one-tailed</i> test; a non-directional hypothesis uses a two-tailed test.","",""],"related":["Karl Popper stressed falsifiability as the mark of a scientific statement.","A directional hypothesis uses a one-tailed test."],"watch":"Statements about hypotheses and H₀ are a steady MSQ theme.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Qualitative approaches","q":"Match the qualitative approach in Column I with its central feature in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Ethnography</td><td>(i) Building a theory from the data through constant comparison</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Phenomenology</td><td>(ii) Studying the stories people tell about their lives</td></tr><tr><td>R. Grounded theory</td><td>(iii) Long-term immersion in a culture-sharing group</td></tr><tr><td>S. Narrative research</td><td>(iv) Describing the essence of a lived experience</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(iv), Q-(iii), R-(ii), S-(i)","P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)"],"answer":2,"why":"Ethnography means immersion in a culture. Phenomenology captures the essence of a lived experience. Grounded theory (Glaser & Strauss, 1967) builds theory from data. Narrative research studies life stories. All four are qualitative, but they differ in focus: a culture, an experience, a theory or a life story.","whyNot":["Ethnography and phenomenology are swapped.","Phenomenology does not use constant comparison; grounded theory does.","","Ethnography is not about individual life stories."],"related":["Content analysis draws themes or categories from text.","A case study describes one person or event in depth."],"watch":"Qualitative-approach matches are increasing in recent papers. Know the core feature of each approach.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Developmental designs","q":"Match the research design in Column I with its description in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Cross-sectional</td><td>(i) The same group is tested repeatedly over several years</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Longitudinal</td><td>(ii) An in-depth study of one individual</td></tr><tr><td>R. Cross-sequential</td><td>(iii) Different age groups are compared at one point in time</td></tr><tr><td>S. Case study</td><td>(iv) Several age groups are each followed over a period of time</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(i), Q-(iii), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)","P-(iv), Q-(i), R-(iii), S-(ii)"],"answer":1,"why":"Cross-sectional means different ages at one time (iii). Longitudinal means the same people over time (i). Cross-sequential means several age groups followed over time (iv). A case study is one individual (ii). The cross-sequential design combines the speed of the cross-sectional design with the depth of the longitudinal design, and helps separate age effects from cohort effects.","whyNot":["Cross-sectional and longitudinal are swapped.","","Longitudinal is not the combined design.","Cross-sectional is not the combined design."],"related":["Cross-sequential is also called the sequential or cohort-sequential design (Schaie).","Cohort effects affect cross-sectional designs; attrition affects longitudinal designs."],"watch":"Scenario questions on developmental designs come regularly. Practise identifying the design from a description.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Focus groups","q":"Which of the following is/are features of a <b>focus group</b>?","options":["It is useful for exploring opinions and experiences in depth","Interaction among participants itself generates data","Its results can be statistically generalised to the whole population","A small group discussion guided by a moderator"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"A focus group is a guided discussion of about 6–10 people. The interaction between them brings out views that one-to-one interviews may miss. It is especially useful at the start of research, for example to find out what issues matter before building a questionnaire.","whyNot":["","","Small, purposive groups do not allow statistical generalisation.",""],"related":["Focus groups are often used in the qualitative phase of mixed-methods studies.","Group dynamics, such as a dominant member, can bias the discussion."],"watch":"Focus groups are named in the revised syllabus list of data-collection methods. Expect a feature-based question.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Experimental method","q":"Which of the following statement(s) about a <b>true experiment</b> is/are correct?","options":["It always has higher external validity than a field study","Participants are randomly assigned to conditions","It allows a cause-and-effect conclusion","The independent variable is manipulated by the researcher"],"answer":[1,2,3],"why":"A true experiment manipulates the IV, randomly assigns participants and controls other variables. This is what makes causal conclusions possible. Manipulation and random assignment together rule out other explanations, which is the scientific basis of a causal claim.","whyNot":["Laboratory experiments are often <i>lower</i> in external validity than field studies, because the setting is artificial.","","",""],"related":["A quasi-experiment has no random assignment.","Correlational research cannot establish causation."],"watch":"Words like ‘always’ and ‘never’ usually signal the wrong MSQ option.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Quasi-independent variables","q":"Which of the following variable(s) CANNOT be randomly assigned to participants and is/are therefore studied as quasi-independent (subject) variables?","options":["Method of instruction used in a class","Age","Gender","Handedness"],"answer":[1,2,3],"why":"Gender, age and handedness are characteristics participants already have. A method of instruction can be assigned by the researcher. Such variables are fixed characteristics of people, so the researcher can only select participants on them, not assign them. This makes cause-and-effect conclusions weaker.","whyNot":["Teaching method can be randomly assigned, so it can be a true IV.","","",""],"related":["Studies using subject variables cannot prove causation.","Such comparisons are called ex post facto designs."],"watch":"The true vs quasi-experimental distinction is a common source of tricky MSQs.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Experimental control","q":"Consider the statements:<br>P. A double-blind design controls both participants’ expectations and experimenter bias.<br>Q. In a double-blind design, the participants know whether they are receiving the treatment.<br>Which of the following is correct?","options":["Only P is correct","Only Q is correct","Both P and Q are correct","Neither P nor Q is correct"],"answer":0,"why":"In a double-blind design neither the participants nor the experimenters who deal with them know the group assignment. So P is correct and Q is wrong. Because no one involved knows who received the treatment, neither participants’ hopes nor the researcher’s expectations can bias the results.","whyNot":["","Q is false, because participants are blind.","Q is false.","P is true."],"related":["A triple-blind design also keeps the data analysts unaware.","Blinding is used along with a placebo control."],"watch":"Statement-pair (P–Q) questions are now a standard format. Judge each statement on its own.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Learning & Memory","topic":"Forgetting","q":"Riya learnt French in school and is now learning Spanish. She finds that Spanish words come to mind when she tries to recall French words. This is an example of:","options":["Proactive interference","Retroactive interference","Trace decay","Encoding failure"],"answer":1,"why":"Retroactive interference means <i>new</i> learning (Spanish) disrupts recall of <i>old</i> learning (French). Remember: retro = backward. The new Spanish words block access to the older French words learnt for similar meanings.","whyNot":["Proactive interference is old learning disrupting new learning.","","Decay is fading with time, not caused by other learning.","Encoding failure means the information never entered memory."],"related":["Both types of interference are strongest when the materials are similar.","Ebbinghaus’ forgetting curve shows rapid early forgetting."],"watch":"Interference scenarios come up often. Remember: retro = new disrupts old.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Internal validity","q":"Match the threat to internal validity in Column I with its example in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. History</td><td>(i) Taking a pre-test makes participants do better on the post-test</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Maturation</td><td>(ii) A national exam result announced between pre-test and post-test changes students’ mood</td></tr><tr><td>R. Testing effect</td><td>(iii) Children selected for very low scores score higher on retest without any treatment</td></tr><tr><td>S. Statistical regression</td><td>(iv) Children’s reading improves over a year simply because they grow older</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(ii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(iii)","P-(iv), Q-(ii), R-(i), S-(iii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)"],"answer":3,"why":"History is an outside event (ii). Maturation is natural growth (iv). The testing effect comes from taking the pre-test (i). Regression means extreme scores move towards the mean (iii). Each threat offers another explanation for a change in scores, so without control we cannot be sure the treatment caused the change.","whyNot":["Maturation is not about the pre-test.","History is an outside event, not growth.","The testing effect and regression are swapped.",""],"related":["Campbell & Stanley listed these threats (1963).","Other threats: selection, attrition (mortality), instrumentation."],"watch":"Validity and confounding are tested every year in some form. Threats to validity are a likely next step.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Schools of psychology","q":"Which of the following pair(s) is/are <b>correctly</b> matched?","options":["Ivan Pavlov – Psychoanalysis","Abraham Maslow – Humanistic psychology","Max Wertheimer – Gestalt psychology","Edward Titchener – Structuralism"],"answer":[1,2,3],"why":"Wertheimer founded Gestalt, Maslow is a humanistic psychologist and Titchener led structuralism. Pavlov was a physiologist whose work on conditioning gave behaviourism its experimental method.","whyNot":["Pavlov studied classical conditioning. His work was the foundation of behaviourism, not psychoanalysis.","","",""],"related":["Freud founded psychoanalysis.","Wundt is the father of experimental psychology."],"watch":"‘Correctly matched pairs’ MSQs reward thorough revision of names.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Cognition","topic":"Intelligence","q":"Which of the following is/are part of Howard Gardner’s theory of <b>multiple intelligences</b>?","options":["Linguistic intelligence","Practical intelligence","Logical-mathematical intelligence","Fluid intelligence"],"answer":[0,2],"why":"Linguistic and logical-mathematical intelligence are among Gardner’s multiple intelligences (others include spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal and naturalistic). Gardner argued that intelligence is not one general ability but several relatively independent abilities.","whyNot":["","Practical intelligence belongs to Sternberg’s triarchic theory.","","Fluid intelligence is part of Cattell’s fluid–crystallised theory."],"related":["Sternberg’s triarchic theory: analytical, creative and practical intelligence.","Cattell divided intelligence into fluid and crystallised."],"watch":"Theories of intelligence come regularly in analogy and MSQ forms. Keep Gardner and Sternberg clearly separate.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Research ethics","q":"To include 10-year-old school children in a study, the researcher should ideally obtain:","options":["Only the children’s verbal agreement","Permission from the school principal only","No consent, if the responses are anonymous","Informed consent from parents/guardians and assent from the children"],"answer":3,"why":"Minors cannot give legal informed consent. So the guardian’s consent is needed, along with the child’s own assent (agreement) in age-appropriate language. Assent respects the child’s own wish to take part, while parental consent provides the legal and ethical permission.","whyNot":["A child’s agreement alone is not enough.","Institutional permission does not replace parental consent.","Anonymity does not remove the need for consent.",""],"related":["Ethics committees (IRB/IEC) approve research before it begins.","Confidentiality and the right to withdraw also apply to children."],"watch":"Ethics questions in GATE stay practical and scenario-based.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Normal curve","q":"Which of the following is/are properties of the <b>normal distribution</b>?","options":["Mean, median and mode are equal","Its tails approach but never touch the baseline","It is symmetrical about the mean","About 68% of scores lie within ±1 SD of the mean"],"answer":[0,1,2,3],"why":"The normal curve is symmetrical, its mean, median and mode coincide, about 68% of scores fall within ±1 SD, and its tails are asymptotic: they never touch the baseline. Because of these fixed properties, the normal curve lets us convert any score into a z-score and find the percentage of people above or below it.","whyNot":["","","",""],"related":["IQ scores are standardised to a normal curve with mean 100 and SD 15.","Kurtosis of the normal curve is called mesokurtic."],"watch":"Normal-curve properties and percentages are asked almost every year. Learn the 68–95–99.7 rule.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Personality","topic":"Rogers","q":"According to Carl Rogers, a large gap between a person’s self-concept (real self) and ideal self is called:","options":["Incongruence","Inferiority complex","Cognitive dissonance","Identity diffusion"],"answer":0,"why":"For Rogers, incongruence between self-concept and experience, or between real and ideal self, produces anxiety and maladjustment. Congruence is the healthy state. When people receive love only under certain conditions (conditions of worth), they deny experiences that do not fit their self-image. This creates the gap Rogers called incongruence.","whyNot":["","The inferiority complex is Adler’s concept.","Cognitive dissonance (Festinger) is the tension between conflicting cognitions.","Identity diffusion is Marcia’s identity status."],"related":["Conditions of worth come from conditional positive regard.","Rogers’ client-centred therapy offers empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard."],"watch":"Rogers’ concepts are asked regularly, and personality questions are increasing. Revise real self, ideal self and congruence.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Development","topic":"Moral development","q":"According to Kohlberg, a child who obeys rules mainly to avoid punishment is at the:","options":["Conventional level","Pre-conventional level","Post-conventional level","Formal operational level"],"answer":1,"why":"At the pre-conventional level (Stage 1: obedience and punishment orientation), right and wrong are judged by the consequences for oneself. At this level, morality comes from outside: an act is ‘bad’ if it gets punished and ‘good’ if it is rewarded.","whyNot":["The conventional level means following social rules and laws to gain approval or keep order.","","The post-conventional level means judging by universal ethical principles.","Formal operations is a Piagetian stage of cognitive development."],"related":["Kohlberg used moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma.","Gilligan criticised Kohlberg for neglecting an ‘ethic of care’."],"watch":"Kohlberg’s levels are asked regularly. Any of the three levels can come.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Deception","q":"Under the APA Ethics Code, deception in research is permitted only when which of the following condition(s) is/are met?","options":["Participants are debriefed as early as possible","It is justified by the study’s significant value","It conceals risks that are expected to cause severe distress","Effective non-deceptive alternatives are not feasible"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":"APA Standard 8.07 allows deception only if it is justified, no alternative is available and participants are debriefed promptly. It is never allowed if it hides risks of pain or severe distress. Deception is allowed only as a last resort, and the participant must learn the truth as soon as possible.","whyNot":["","","Deception about risks that may cause pain or severe distress is never allowed.",""],"related":["Using confederates is a common form of deception.","Falsifying data is never acceptable under any condition."],"watch":"Deception and research ethics are tested repeatedly. One ethics question is definitely expected.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MSQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Mixed methods","q":"Which of the following statement(s) about <b>mixed-methods</b> research is/are correct?","options":["It combines quantitative and qualitative data within one study","In an explanatory sequential design, the qualitative phase comes first","It helps triangulate and strengthen findings","Pragmatism is commonly its philosophical worldview"],"answer":[0,2,3],"why":"Mixed methods integrate quantitative and qualitative strands, are usually based on pragmatism, and allow triangulation. The tricky option swaps the order of the explanatory and exploratory designs.","whyNot":["","In the explanatory sequential design the <i>quantitative</i> phase comes first and the qualitative phase explains it. Qualitative-first is the exploratory design.","",""],"related":["Creswell’s worldviews: postpositivist, constructivist, transformative and pragmatic.","Triangulation means using multiple methods or sources to cross-check findings."],"watch":"Mixed methods is a rising area. Know its definition, worldview and purpose.","level":"H"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Quasi-experiment","q":"A researcher introduces a yoga programme only in a private school and compares the stress scores of its students with those of a government school. Students were not randomly assigned to schools. The design is:","options":["True experimental design","Quasi-experimental design","Correlational design","Single-subject design"],"answer":1,"why":"A treatment is introduced, but the groups already exist and were not randomly assigned. This is the defining feature of a quasi-experiment. Because the groups were not formed randomly, the two schools may already differ in many ways, such as income or teaching quality. This weakens causal conclusions.","whyNot":["A true experiment requires random assignment.","","In a correlational design nothing is introduced or manipulated.","A single-subject design studies one participant repeatedly."],"related":["The non-equivalent control group design is the most common quasi-experiment.","Quasi-experiments are lower in internal validity than true experiments."],"watch":"Quasi-experiments are named in the revised syllabus. Expect a question telling them apart from true experiments.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Perspectives","q":"Match the perspective in Column I with its main focus in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Biological</td><td>(i) Influence of culture and social context on behaviour</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Cognitive</td><td>(ii) Genes, the brain, hormones and the nervous system</td></tr><tr><td>R. Sociocultural</td><td>(iii) Behaviours that helped our ancestors survive and reproduce</td></tr><tr><td>S. Evolutionary</td><td>(iv) Thinking, memory and information processing</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(ii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(iii)","P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(iii), S-(i)","P-(ii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(iii)"],"answer":3,"why":"The biological perspective studies the body’s systems (ii). The cognitive perspective studies mental processes (iv). The sociocultural perspective studies culture and social context (i). The evolutionary perspective studies adaptive behaviour (iii). Each perspective explains the same behaviour at a different level, from genes and the brain up to culture.","whyNot":["Cognitive and sociocultural are swapped.","Biological and evolutionary are swapped.","Sociocultural and evolutionary are swapped.",""],"related":["Modern psychology often combines perspectives, for example the biopsychosocial model.","The evolutionary perspective draws on Darwin’s natural selection."],"watch":"Perspectives keep returning as match questions. Revise each perspective’s main focus.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Research Methods","topic":"Mixed methods","q":"A researcher first holds focus groups with teachers to identify themes of classroom stress. She then uses these themes to build a questionnaire administered to 600 teachers. This is an example of:","options":["Convergent parallel design","Explanatory sequential design","Exploratory sequential design","Embedded experimental design"],"answer":2,"why":"In an exploratory sequential design the qualitative phase comes first to explore themes, and its results shape the quantitative phase (the survey). The first phase explores the topic in depth, and the second phase tests on a large sample whether what was found holds more widely.","whyNot":["In a convergent design both kinds of data are collected at the same time and compared.","An explanatory design is the reverse: quantitative first, then qualitative to explain it.","","In an embedded design one data type plays a supporting role inside a larger design."],"related":["Creswell’s three core mixed-methods designs: convergent, explanatory sequential and exploratory sequential.","Pragmatism is the usual worldview behind mixed methods."],"watch":"Mixed-methods questions are rising year after year. Learn the three core designs.","level":"M"},
    {"type":"NAT","marks":2,"subject":"Statistics","topic":"Standard scores","q":"On a test with mean 60 and standard deviation 8, a student scores 72. Her T-score (mean 50, SD 10) is ________ (answer in integer).","answer":{"min":65,"max":65},"why":"z = (72 − 60) ÷ 8 = 1.5. T = 50 + 10z = 50 + 15 = <b>65</b>. Converting a score to a z-score first and then to a T-score lets us compare performance across tests that use different scales.","related":["The z-score formula is z = (X − M) ÷ SD.","Stanines have mean 5 and SD about 2; Wechsler IQ has mean 100 and SD 15."],"watch":"Standard-score conversions are a natural NAT. Practise z → T → IQ conversions.","whyNot":[],"level":"H"},
    {"type":"MCQ","marks":2,"subject":"Foundations of Psychology","topic":"Schools of psychology","q":"Match the school of psychology in Column I with its central idea in Column II.<table class='match'><tr><th>Column I</th><th>Column II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Structuralism</td><td>(i) Mental processes help the organism adapt to its environment</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Functionalism</td><td>(ii) Only observable behaviour is the proper subject of psychology</td></tr><tr><td>R. Gestalt</td><td>(iii) Consciousness is analysed into basic elements through introspection</td></tr><tr><td>S. Behaviourism</td><td>(iv) The whole is different from the sum of its parts</td></tr></table>","options":["P-(iii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(i), Q-(iii), R-(iv), S-(ii)","P-(iii), Q-(iv), R-(i), S-(ii)","P-(ii), Q-(i), R-(iv), S-(iii)"],"answer":0,"why":"Structuralism breaks consciousness into elements (iii). Functionalism studies the adaptive purpose of the mind (i). Gestalt stresses wholes (iv). Behaviourism studies only observable behaviour (ii). These four schools developed largely as reactions to each other: functionalism and Gestalt reacted against structuralism, and behaviourism rejected the study of consciousness altogether.","whyNot":["","Structuralism and functionalism are swapped.","Functionalism is not about wholes, and Gestalt is not about adaptation.","Structuralism did not restrict psychology to behaviour."],"related":["Titchener coined the terms structuralism and functionalism.","Gestalt arose as a reaction against structuralism."],"watch":"Match-the-following is the most common 2-mark format. Solve it with the one pair you are surest of.","level":"M"}
  ]
};
